      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I think the idea of not running the scene in the first place and have the scripts for upload run without play mode would be very nice. This would also mean the scene doesn't have to be cleared/unloaded. (this is good because if the scene us unloaded, and at any point something triggers the project save, all scenes will have to be re-added by hand from their save files if the editor crashes. In my case they are all over the place so I wouldn't be able to remember them all. This happened allot to me with bakery wehre it did sorta the same before they fixed said crash) Also looking at the scripts themselves, there is no real reason for it to run in play mode. Except for the GUI. And would you look at that new GUI the dev's just made for the SDK, take it a step further I'd say :)",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2019-08-21T08:36:29.858Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "When uploading a scene, the uploader will keep running whatever's currently present in the Editor after clicking Upload. Then, once the upload has finished, and if Upload Image is selected, the uploader will take an image and begin uploading it. If either of these fails, the upload breaks. \n\nHowever, running a scene in the background can be performance intensive. It's my anecdotal experience that world uploads are more likely to fail with complicated scenes due to the CPU load of running them and the uploader. Also, setting up the right screenshot can be quite difficult when you need to wait for the upload to conclude before it's taken.\n\nI've gotten into the habit of doing two things manually which I think ought to be part of the current upload process.\n - Take an image *when* the Upload button is pressed. \n - Delete the scene contents *before* the Upload is started.\n\nRemoving the scene contents reduces CPU load quite a lot in some cases, as Unity no longer has to render the scene *in the background* and run all the physics, lighting, and audio processing. Plus, if an upload image is taken before the upload starts, there's no reason to run the scene.",
